Here is a complete, copy-paste solution using standard HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.

I have used a **Flexbox wrapper** approach. This is the modern best practice because it keeps the icon and input aligned perfectly without using tricky absolute positioning, and it ensures the icon is accessible (keyboard navigable).

### The Code

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Search Input with Icon</title>
    <style>
        /* 1. The Container */
        .search-wrapper {
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
            width: 300px; /* Adjust width as needed */
            border: 1px solid #ccc;
            border-radius: 8px;
            padding: 5px 10px;
            background-color: #fff;
            transition: border-color 0.3s;
        }

        .search-wrapper:focus-within {
            border-color: #007bff; /* Highlight color when input is focused */
            box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(0, 123, 255, 0.1);
        }

        /* 2. The Input Field */
        .search-input {
            flex-grow: 1; /* Takes up all available space */
            border: none;
            outline: none;
            padding: 8px;
            font-size: 16px;
            background: transparent;
        }

        /* 3. The Clickable Icon Button */
        .search-btn {
            background: none;
            border: none;
            cursor: pointer;
            padding: 8px;
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
            justify-content: center;
            border-radius: 4px;
            transition: background-color 0.2s;
        }

        .search-btn:hover {
            background-color: #f0f0f0;
        }

        /* 4. The SVG Icon */
        .search-icon {
            width: 20px;
            height: 20px;
            fill: #555; /* Icon color */
        }

        .search-btn:hover .search-icon {
            fill: #007bff; /* Icon color on hover */
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <!-- HTML Structure -->
    <div class="search-wrapper">
        <input type="text" class="search-input" id="myInput" placeholder="Search...">

        <!-- Use a button for accessibility, not just a raw SVG -->
        <button type="button" class="search-btn" id="searchIcon" aria-label="Search">
            <svg class="search-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                <path d="M21.71 20.29L18 16.61A9 9 0 1 0 16.61 18l3.68 3.68a1 1 0 0 0 1.42 0 1 1 0 0 0 0-1.39zM11 18a7 7 0 1 1 7-7 7 7 0 0 1-7 7z"/>
            </svg>
        </button>
    </div>

    <script>
        // 1. Select elements
        const searchBtn = document.getElementById('searchIcon');
        const inputField = document.getElementById('myInput');

        // 2. Define your function
        function handleSearch() {
            const query = inputField.value.trim();

            if (query) {
                console.log("Searching for:", query);
                alert("Searching for: " + query);
                // Call your API or navigation logic here
            } else {
                console.log("Input is empty");
            }
        }

        // 3. Add Click Event Listener
        searchBtn.addEventListener('click', handleSearch);

        // Optional: Trigger search when pressing "Enter" key
        inputField.addEventListener('keypress', function (e) {
            if (e.key === 'Enter') {
                handleSearch();
            }
        });
    </script>

</body>
</html>
```

### Key Details Explained

1.  **The Wrapper (`.search-wrapper`)**:
    *   We wrap both the input and the icon in a `div`.
    *   We give the **wrapper** the border and background color, not the input itself. This makes it look like the icon is "inside" the input field seamlessly.
    *   `display: flex` aligns the input and the button side-by-side.

2.  **The Button (`<button>`)**:
    *   **Do not** put the `onClick` directly on the `<svg>`. SVGs are not natively focusable or clickable for screen readers.
    *   Wrap the SVG in a `<button type="button">`. This makes it keyboard accessible (users can tab to it and press Enter).
    *   We remove the default button styles (`background: none`, `border: none`) so it looks like a pure icon.

3.  **The JavaScript**:
    *   We select the button and add an `addEventListener`.
    *   Inside the function, we grab the value using `inputField.value`.
    *   I added an extra feature: **Pressing "Enter"**. Usually, users expect to be able to hit Enter in the input box to trigger the same search function.

4.  **Accessibility (`aria-label`)**:
    *   The button has `aria-label="Search"`. Since the button only contains an image (SVG), screen readers need this text to know what the button does.

